---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Brand guidelines"
  description="How the Next Step Studios logo, colours and typefaces should be used, including the transparent master and the dark-surface treatment."
  path="/brand"
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="page-head-stack">
        <p class="eyebrow">Brand guidelines</p>
        <h1 class="display display--page">Use the mark without breaking it.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A short, practical set of rules for the logo, the palette and the type. They exist so the
          studio looks like one thing wherever it appears — not to make anyone read a manual.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/downloads/next-step-studios-brand-kit.zip" download>
            Download the assets (.zip)
          </a>
          <a class="btn" href="/archive">See the archive</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="brand-grid">
        <article class="prose brand-prose">
          <h2>The logo</h2>
          <p>
            The mark is a detailed emblem supplied as a transparent raster master at 1024px, with
            smaller files at 512, 256 and 128. Use the largest size the placement allows and let it
            scale down, never up: enlarging a raster mark is the fastest way to make it look cheap.
          </p>

          <h3>Two treatments, not two logos</h3>
          <p>
            The standard transparent file is for light surfaces. On a dark surface the black ninja
            and the black “STUDIOS” line disappear, so a second treatment adds a light outline
            grown from the artwork itself. Use the light-outline file on dark, and the standard
            file on light. They are the same mark.
          </p>

          <h3>Clear space</h3>
          <p>
            Leave clear space around the mark equal to at least one quarter of its height on every
            side. Nothing — text, other logos, a border — enters that space.
          </p>

          <h3>Minimum size</h3>
          <p>
            24px high on screen, 10mm high in print. Below that the detail inside the emblem stops
            resolving and the mark becomes a smudge.
          </p>

          <h3>Backgrounds</h3>
          <p>
            The PNGs are genuinely transparent, so the mark can sit on any flat colour. Use the
            light treatment on dark backgrounds and the standard treatment on light ones. Never
            place the mark on a busy photograph, and never add a plate behind it to make it work.
          </p>

          <h3>JPG files</h3>
          <p>
            JPG cannot store transparency. The JPGs in the kit are therefore flattened on the
            studio's dark base <code>#07080B</code>, using the light-outline treatment. If you need
            the mark on white, use the transparent PNG over white rather than a flattened JPG.
          </p>

          <h2>What not to do</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Do not recolour, gradient or outline the mark.</li>
            <li>Do not stretch, squash or rotate it.</li>
            <li>Do not add effects, shadows or glow.</li>
            <li>Do not crop the emblem or place text inside its clear space.</li>
            <li>Do not use it to suggest endorsement, partnership or sponsorship.</li>
            <li>Do not use the transparent file on dark and call the black areas “subtle”.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Type</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Space Grotesk</strong> for display headings, <strong>Inter</strong> for body text
            and <strong>JetBrains Mono</strong> for code, labels and figures. All three are open
            licence and loaded from the site itself, never a third-party font host.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class="brand-aside">
          <div class="panel pad">
            <p class="label">Palette</p>
            <ul class="palette-list">
              <li><span class="swatch-chip" style="background:#07080B" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Base</span><code>#07080B</code></li>
              <li><span class="swatch-chip" style="background:#E9EEF7" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Ink</span><code>#E9EEF7</code></li>
              <li><span class="swatch-chip" style="background:#4D8DFF" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Accent</span><code>#4D8DFF</code></li>
              <li><span class="swatch-chip" style="background:#A78BFA" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Violet</span><code>#A78BFA</code></li>
              <li><span class="swatch-chip" style="background:#FF64D8" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Magenta</span><code>#FF64D8</code></li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="panel pad">
            <p class="label">Assets</p>
            <p class="muted">
              PNG, JPG and SVG at 1024, 512, 256 and 128, in both treatments, plus the retired
              emblem and the site's four favicons. One ZIP, no form.
            </p>
            <a class="btn" href="/press">Press kit</a>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="notice notice--neutral">
        <span class="status status--info">Honest limitation</span>
        <p class="notice-body">
          The mark is not a vector redraw. The SVG files embed the transparent PNG at each size so
          layout scales cleanly, but a true vector master is still to be produced. When it is, it
          will be published alongside — not in place of — today's files. The original white-tile
          edition is kept on the <a class="text-link" href="/archive">archive page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
